  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1232 AM MDT Sun Sep 20 2026\n\nMonday and Tuesday will find us in between systems, with a short-\nlived upper ridge in place as the previous low moves east, and a \nsecond low pushes in from California. On Monday, increasing westerly \nwinds aloft will force drier air into the area. Other than lingering \nshowers over the SE plains early in the day, we should remain mostly \ndry with highs in the 70s. On Tuesday, flow aloft turns more \nsouthwesterly as a trough deepens over the western CONUS, letting \nmore moisture back into the higher terrain. Afternoon showers and \nstorms will be more likely over the mountains, especially further \nwest, while the plains only get some isolated activity.\n\nLooking later in the week, models still show a lot of variability \nbetween solutions as well as individual runs. General consensus \nshows the upper low pushing northeast around midweek, with hints at \na high pressure center building in over the region by Friday, though \nthat solution remains uncertain. Overall, we should be seeing a slow \nincrease in temperatures, along with daily chances for showers and \nthunderstorms centered over the higher terrain and our southern \nborder.",
